{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Build Titan's Professional Financial Model Skeleton",
  "scenario": "You're a junior analyst at a Mumbai brokerage tasked with setting up the foundational architecture for Titan Company's DCF valuation model. Your senior, Karthik, has reviewed your first draft and sent it back — the maths is correct, but the model is unauditable: colour-coding is missing, assumptions are buried in formulas, and there's no error-checking. Before you forecast a single rupee, you must rebuild the workbench with proper discipline: blue inputs, black formulas, named ranges, and integrity checks.",
  "instructions": "Set up a professional analyst's workbench for Titan: colour-code inputs and formulas correctly, create named ranges for key assumptions, build a master error-check formula, and ensure every assumption cell is labelled and referenced using absolute references.",
  "learning_objective": "Build a clean financial model skeleton with colour-coded inputs (blue), formulas (black), absolute references, named ranges, and error-checking logic that any stranger can audit in under 30 seconds.",
  "hints": [
    "Inputs (assumptions you choose) go in blue cells with absolute references ($B$10 format); formulas (what Excel calculates) stay black and reference the blue cells using $ anchors so they don't shift when copied.",
    "A named range like 'TaxRate' lets you write =EBT*TaxRate instead of =EBT*Assumptions!$B$12 — far more readable. Create one by selecting the cell, clicking the Name Box, typing the name, pressing Enter.",
    "A master check formula like =IF(SUM(check1,check2,check3)=0,\"✓ ALL CLEAR\",\"✗ ERROR\") returns zero when all sub-checks pass (all assumptions are sensible) and screams a warning otherwise — it's your smoke detector."
  ],
